# Android release build (signing and R8)

Sibling of [android-app](android-app.mdc); deployment and on-device verification are in the
[android-deploy](../../skills/android-deploy/SKILL.md) skill.

A release build fails differently from a debug build: it produces an APK that installs and runs,
with one feature quietly not working. Both rules below were verified by reading the R8 mapping,
not by assumption.

## R8 may rename enum constants, and `Enum.name` returns the compiled name

`Enum.name` returns the **compiled** field name. R8 is free to rename those fields, so code that
persists an enum by name and matches it back can break — silently, with no crash and no log line.

```kotlin
// Written in debug, works. In release, it.name is "i", never "Wiegand", so every stored
// override stops matching and quietly falls back to auto-detection.
DeviceProfile.entries.firstOrNull { it.name == storedOverride }
```

Verified by building with `proguard-rules.pro` emptied and reading
`app/build/outputs/mapping/release/mapping.txt`:

```
without keep rules:  DeviceProfile.Wiegand   -> i
   with keep rules:  DeviceProfile.Wiegand   -> Wiegand
```

Keep the constants:

```proguard
-keepclassmembers enum com.example.data.** {
    public static **[] values();
    public static ** valueOf(java.lang.String);
    **[] $VALUES;
    public *;
}
```

Better still, don't persist `Enum.name` at all — store an explicit stable key (`@SerialName`, or
an `id` field you control) so the on-disk value is decoupled from the field name. Any reflective
lookup by name is suspect under R8: enum constants, class names, `Class.forName`.

**The name can reach a key, not just a value — and that version is louder.** The example
above loses one stored setting. An app that builds *key names* from the enum loses a whole
namespace at once:

```kotlin
// Release reads "seen.child1.a" where debug wrote "seen.child1.homework".
stringSetPreferencesKey("seen.$childId.${feed.name.lowercase()}")
```

Nothing throws — the read just returns empty. If a *separate* flag records that the app has
already initialised (`seeded.<child>`, keyed on something R8 did not touch), the two now
disagree: the app believes it has seen this data before, while every lookup says it has seen
nothing. One measured consequence was a notification per item across an entire academic
year — roughly 350 at once — from an app whose whole value was notifying sparingly.

Same fix, and it is a rename with no migration when the key values match what the old code
produced:

```kotlin
enum class Feed(val key: String) { HOMEWORK("homework"), NOTICE("notice"), … }
```

Read it back through a lookup on `key` rather than `valueOf`: a `PendingIntent` or a stored
record written by the previous version outlives the upgrade, so accept the old spelling too.

### Whether it actually renames is per-project — verify, don't assume either way

A second Kotlin/Compose app (Isai, AGP 8.7.2, `proguard-android-optimize.txt`,
`isMinifyEnabled` + `isShrinkResources`, **no** enum keep rules) persisted
`StationCategory.name` into DataStore and matched it back with
`entries.firstOrNull { it.name == stored }` — the exact shape above — and the names **survived**:
the release DEX still contains `Talk`. Plausible reason: `.name` is read in the same module, so
R8 can see the names being observed. That is a hypothesis; only the outcome was verified.

So the two projects disagree, and neither result generalises. Do not conclude "R8 renamed it" or
"R8 kept it" from experience — the keep rule above costs nothing, and the check below is cheap.
Storing a key you control sidesteps the question entirely, which is why it stays the advice.

**Do not assume kotlinx-serialization needs keep rules for this.** It does not, and believing
otherwise is folklore worth not repeating. Its artifacts ship consumer rules, so generated
serializers survive:

```
without keep rules:  SavedDevice$$serializer -> x1.K     renamed, NOT stripped
```

Renaming is harmless there because generated code references the serializer directly rather
than by name. The enum is the real hazard.

### The explicit key only helps while there is exactly one of it

Replacing `Enum.name` with a key you control fixes the R8 hazard and creates a
new one: the key is now a value someone can retype. A second declaration of the
same enum, in a module that cannot see the first, compiles perfectly and writes
different strings into the same storage. R8 is not involved; this breaks in debug
too, and the R8 checks above will not catch it.

**Verified case.** A chess app declared `PuzzleModes` in `:app` while the data
layer already had one. The app's copy spelled a key `"thinkfirst"` where the
stored value was `"think_first"`, and added a `BY_ID` constant the data layer
never defined. Everything compiled; the writes went into the user's saved puzzle
history with keys nothing would ever read back. The module had a `Keys.kt`
existing for exactly this reason, one import away.

- **Persisted keys live in one module, and every other module imports them.** The
  module that owns the storage owns the keys; a UI module declaring a storage key
  is the bug, not the spelling.
- **Grep before declaring.** `grep -rn "think_first\|PuzzleMode" --include=*.kt`
  costs seconds and is the only thing that finds a duplicate, since the compiler
  is happy with both.
- **A constant with no reader is a signal.** The invented `BY_ID` had no
  counterpart in the data layer at all, which a "does every key round-trip"
  test would have caught:
  ```kotlin
  @Test fun everyModeKeyIsKnownToTheDataLayer() =
      PuzzleMode.entries.forEach { assertNotNull(Keys.puzzleMode(it.key)) }
  ```
- **Suspect this after parallel work**, the same way as a duplicated constants
  object: see [testing](../core/testing.mdc#the-same-hazard-without-a-rebuilt-object-two-definitions-of-the-same-constant),
  where the drawn board colours and the tested ones had forked the same way.

## A library can add a permission you never declared, and only the APK shows it

Manifest merge folds in the `<uses-permission>` entries of every dependency.
Nothing in your source mentions them, so reading the repo, grepping, and
reviewing the diff all report a clean manifest while the shipped app asks for
more than you think.

**Verified case.** An offline Android app whose About screen promised "nothing
leaves your phone" shipped with `ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE`, `WAKE_LOCK`,
`RECEIVE_BOOT_COMPLETED` and `FOREGROUND_SERVICE`, all contributed by
WorkManager for a single unconstrained daily reminder. `ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE`
directly contradicted the privacy claim. None of the four appeared anywhere in
the source manifest.

Read the permissions off the **built artifact**, which is the only place they
exist:

```powershell
$apk = (Get-Content app\build\outputs\apk\release\output-metadata.json | ConvertFrom-Json).elements[0].outputFile
aapt2 dump permissions app\build\outputs\apk\release\$apk
```

Remove the ones you do not use, in the source manifest, with a comment saying
which dependency contributes each:

```xml
<!-- WorkManager contributes these; the daily job is unconstrained and need not
     survive a reboot, so none is used. Re-check the APK if that changes. -->
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE" tools:node="remove" />
```

- **Put the check in the release gate, not in a reviewer's head.** Fail the build
  on any permission outside an allowlist, alongside the existing no-`INTERNET`
  and signature checks.
- **A removal is a claim that the feature does not need it.** If a worker later
  gains a network constraint or a foreground service, `tools:node="remove"` turns
  it into a silent runtime failure, so delete the line rather than working around
  it, and re-dump the APK.
- **This matters most where a privacy claim is part of the product.** An unused
  permission is a store-review and trust finding whatever the reason for it, and
  "a library added it" is not a defence a user can see.

## Verify R8 statically, not by launching the app

Launching a release build tells you nothing about a code path you did not exercise, and the
failure mode here is a feature that silently no-ops. Two static probes; the second is the one
that always answers.

**Probe 1 — the mapping file.** Cheap when it says anything:

```bash
grep -E '^com\.example\.data\.(MyModel|MyEnum)' app/build/outputs/mapping/release/mapping.txt
grep -E '^\s+com\.example\.data\.MyEnum ' app/build/outputs/mapping/release/mapping.txt
```

A name mapped to itself (`Wiegand -> Wiegand`) is kept; mapped to a short token (`-> i`) is not.

**But absence of a line proves nothing.** In Isai's 42 MB mapping, both enums appear as a bare
class line with **no member lines at all**:

```
fm.isai.radio.data.StationCategory -> h3.F:
fm.isai.radio.data.RecordingQuality -> h3.D:
```

Nothing indented follows either. Read literally that looks like "everything was stripped"; the
constants were in fact all present in the DEX. A silent-empty grep is the worst possible answer
to a silent-failure question, so don't stop here.

**Probe 2 — count the name strings in the DEX.** `Enum.name` comes from the string literal passed
to the `java.lang.Enum` constructor, so a surviving name is a string in the DEX:

```python
import json, zipfile
out = 'app/build/outputs/apk/release/'
z = zipfile.ZipFile(out + json.load(open(out + 'output-metadata.json'))['elements'][0]['outputFile'])
dex = b''.join(z.read(n) for n in z.namelist() if n.endswith('.dex'))
print(dex.count(b'Talk'))     # 1 -> the constant name survived
```

Choose a constant whose name differs from **every** other literal on it, or the count is
ambiguous: `Music("Music")` proves nothing because the label supplies that string anyway.
`Talk("Stories & talk")` is the discriminator — `Talk` can only be the enum's name. Design one
such constant into the check rather than guessing which occurrence is which.

Do all of this whenever `isMinifyEnabled` is turned on, and again after adding anything
reflective.

## Always ship a signed release APK (user rule, Nov-09-2026)

The deliverable of every Android build Claude hands over is a **signed release APK**
(`assembleRelease`), never a debug APK and never an unsigned release. Debug builds are for the
local edit-install loop only.

If the repo has no release key yet, create one in the same change, following the convention
already used across the `D:\GitHub` Android repos:

```powershell
# From the repo root. keytool ships with the JDK (Android Studio's jbr\bin).
keytool -genkeypair -v -keystore release.jks -alias release -keyalg RSA -keysize 2048 -validity 10000 `
  -dname "CN=<App Name>, O=Pandiyaraj Karuppasamy"
```

- Write `keystore.properties` (`storeFile=release.jks`, `storePassword`, `keyAlias=release`,
  `keyPassword`) and gitignore **both** files before the first commit. Generate a random password;
  never print it or put it in a commit, PR or chat.
- Back up both outside the repo straight away, to `%USERPROFILE%\keystores\<repo>-release.jks`
  and `<repo>-keystore.properties` (the folder this machine already uses). Losing the key means
  no installed copy can ever be updated (see below).
- Wire the `signingConfig` as in the next section, then prove it: `apksigner verify --print-certs`
  on the built APK must print the release certificate, not `CN=Android Debug`.

## Signing lives in a gitignored properties file

Release with no `signingConfig` produces an **unsigned APK that cannot be installed** — easy to
miss, because the build succeeds.

```kotlin
val keystoreProps = Properties().apply {
    val f = rootProject.file("keystore.properties")
    if (f.exists()) f.inputStream().use { load(it) }
}
val hasSigning = keystoreProps.getProperty("storeFile") != null &&
    rootProject.file(keystoreProps.getProperty("storeFile")).exists()
```

Guard the whole config on `hasSigning` so a clone without the keystore still *configures* —
failing the configuration phase on a missing local file blocks every task, including `assembleDebug`
and the tests.

**Import `Properties`; do not fully-qualify it.** Inside the `android { }` DSL there is a value
named `java` in scope, and in Kotlin a value shadows a root package of the same name — so the
fully-qualified form fails to resolve, with an error that blames `util`:

```
e: build.gradle.kts:26:34: Unresolved reference: util
e: build.gradle.kts:28:51: Unresolved reference: load
      val keystoreProps = java.util.Properties().apply {
                               ^ Unresolved reference: util
```

Nothing in that names the cause, and `java.util.Properties` is obviously a real class, so the
instinct is to doubt the JDK or the Gradle version. Put `import java.util.Properties` at the top
of the build script and write `Properties()` — which is what the snippet above does, and the
reason it does.

- Gitignore both the keystore and the properties file, then confirm against the **remote**, not
  the working tree: `git ls-tree -r --name-only origin/main | grep -iE '\.jks$|keystore'`.
- **Back the keystore up outside the repo.** Losing it means no future build can update an
  installed copy: Android refuses an update signed with a different key, so every device needs an
  uninstall — taking its saved data with it.
- Debug and release use different keys, so swapping one for the other on a device requires an
  uninstall first. Expect `INSTALL_FAILED_UPDATE_INCOMPATIBLE` and handle it in the deploy
  script rather than rediscovering it each time — though a vendor restriction can surface a
  *different* error at the same step (Isai got `INSTALL_FAILED_USER_RESTRICTED` on MIUI, which
  reads like the release APK is at fault rather than the freshness of the install).
- **Capture the app's saved data before that uninstall.** It is the last moment you can: the
  installed build is still debug, so `run-as` works, and the release build that replaces it is
  not debuggable, so there is no way back in afterwards. The
  [deploy skill](../../skills/android-deploy/SKILL.md) has the command. Read-only, a few
  seconds, and it turns "your settings are gone" into a list you can hand back.

## Name the APK `<appname>_<versionName>.apk`

AGP names every APK after the module: `app-release.apk`, in every repo, at every version. Once
that file is installed, posted to Telegram or handed to someone, nothing about it says which app
or which build it is. Name it in the build instead (user rule, global CLAUDE.md): release
`<appname>_<versionName>.apk`, debug `<appname>_<versionName>-debug.apk`, where the app name is
the Gradle `rootProject.name` and the version is the build's `versionName` (`usbdisplay_0.1.1.apk`,
`learnenglish_0.1.0.apk`). That is the same name the copy in `D:\GitHub\apk` gets. Set it in the
app module's `build.gradle.kts` when creating or touching any Android app:

```kotlin
androidComponents {
    onVariants { variant ->
        val appName = rootProject.name
        variant.outputs.forEach { output ->
            (output as? com.android.build.api.variant.impl.VariantOutputImpl)
                ?.outputFileName
                ?.set(output.versionName.map { v ->
                    if (variant.buildType == "release") "${appName}_$v.apk"
                    else "${appName}_$v-${variant.buildType}.apk"
                })
        }
    }
}
```

Verified on `usbdisplay` (AGP 8.7.2): `assembleRelease` wrote `usbdisplay_0.1.1.apk`, signed, and
`output-metadata.json` recorded the new name in `elements[].outputFile`; `assembleDebug` wrote
`usbdisplay_0.1.1-debug.apk`.

- **Read the file name from `output-metadata.json`**; never hard-code `app-release.apk` in a script,
  task, VS Code task or README. The collect-release-apk init script already reads it, which is why
  it follows the rename with no change. A hard-coded path does not fail loudly: a stale
  `app-release.apk` left in `build/outputs` gets installed or scanned instead of the build you just
  made. Update any install command or README line that still names `app-release.apk`.
- `VariantOutputImpl` is AGP's implementation class; the public `VariantOutput` has no
  `outputFileName`. The cast is the usual way to rename, but it is internal API: if an AGP upgrade
  breaks it, the build fails to compile, which is the safe way for it to fail.
- `${appName}_$v` needs the braces: `$appName_$v` would read a variable called `appName_`.
- Repos that still name APKs after the applicationId, or with the short-lived hyphen form
  (`<repo-folder>-<versionName>.apk`, Nov-09 to Nov-15-2026), move to this name the next time they
  are released.

## A lint failure can be a toolchain mismatch, not a finding

`lintVitalAnalyzeRelease` gates `assembleRelease`, and it fails on a JDK it does not support
with a bare version string and no findings — e.g. `> 25.0.2` on AGP 8.7.2 running under the
JDK 25 that current Android Studio bundles as its JBR.

Recognise it by the shape: no lint report, no rule name, just a number. Build on the JDK the
AGP supports, or upgrade AGP. If the bare number appears at configuration time instead, the
failing compiler is Gradle's own, and `org.gradle.java.home` fixes it; see
[ci-cost](ci-cost.mdc) ("AGP and the JDK"). Disabling the gate is a stopgap: record *why* next to it, or the
next reader assumes the project has lint findings it is hiding:

```kotlin
lint {
    // AGP 8.7.2's lint cannot run on JDK 25; lintVitalAnalyzeRelease dies with a bare
    // "25.0.2". A toolchain incompatibility, not a finding. Re-enable on newer AGP or JDK 17.
    checkReleaseBuilds = false
}
```

## `gradlew` must be committed executable, with LF endings

On Windows, `git init` sets `core.filemode=false`, so a plain `git add` records
`gradlew` as mode `100644`. Everything works locally and the repo is broken for
every Linux or macOS checkout and for CI. Verified Oct-19-2026 under WSL, same
file, three states:

| Committed as | Result |
|---|---|
| mode 644 | exit 126, `Permission denied` |
| mode 755, LF | runs |
| mode 755, CRLF | exit 127, `cannot execute: required file not found` |

The last row is the misleading one: the file exists, the error names the
interpreter line (`bash\r`) as missing. Fix both, then check:

```
git update-index --chmod=+x android/gradlew
git ls-files -s android/gradlew          # must start with 100755
```

Pin the endings in `.gitattributes` (the hub template already has
`gradlew text eol=lf`). The attribute fixes line endings only; it does not fix
the mode, so the `update-index` step is still needed.
